        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(telegram): add request timeout to media file downloads",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\nTelegram media downloads already had a body read idle timeout, but the Bot API file download call did not pass an overall request timeout to `saveRemoteMedia`. A stalled Telegram file endpoint or custom Bot API host could hang before the response/body path that the idle timeout protects.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\nThe shared media runtime already supports `timeoutMs` in addition to `readIdleTimeoutMs`. This PR wires a 120s request timeout into the Telegram media file download call while preserving the existing retry policy, explicit proxy fallback, SSRF policy, and 30s body idle timeout.\n\n## User Impact\nTelegram inbound media downloads now have both connection/request and body-idle protection instead of only body-idle protection.\n\n## Evidence\n- Focused test: `pnpm vitest run extensions/telegram/src/bot/delivery.resolve-media-retry.test.ts` -> 42 passed.\n- Format/lint: `pnpm exec oxfmt --check --threads=1 extensions/telegram/src/bot/delivery.resolve-media.ts extensions/telegram/src/bot/delivery.resolve-media-retry.test.ts && pnpm exec oxlint extensions/telegram/src/bot/delivery.resolve-media.ts extensions/telegram/src/bot/delivery.resolve-media-retry.test.ts` -> passed.\n- Whitespace: `git diff --check` -> clean.\n- Collision check before opening: open PR changed-files query for `extensions/telegram/src/bot/delivery.resolve-media.ts` and `extensions/telegram/src/bot/delivery.resolve-media-retry.test.ts` returned no open PRs.\n\n## Proof\nRegression contract: the explicit-proxy media download test asserts Telegram now calls `saveRemoteMedia` with both `timeoutMs: 120_000` and `readIdleTimeoutMs: 30_000`, preserving the proxy fallback and retry behavior while adding request-level timeout coverage.\n\nControlled Bot API/media runtime stall transcript. The harness uses a redacted Bot API file URL shape, starts a local `node:http` endpoint, lets the endpoint accept the media request, and then withholds response headers. It uses a 500ms timeout so the proof does not wait two minutes; production uses 120s.\n\n```text\n$ pnpm exec tsx --input-type=module - <<'SCRIPT'\n# starts a local Bot API-style file endpoint, calls readRemoteMediaBuffer with timeoutMs=500,\n# and lets the endpoint accept the request while withholding headers\nSCRIPT\nstall-server: accepted media request; intentionally withholding headers\n[fetch-timeout] fetch timeout after 500ms (elapsed 502ms) operation=fetchWithSsrFGuard url=http://127.0.0.1:<port>/bot%3Credacted%3E/file.bin\nmedia-runtime: aborted stalled request after 577ms\nmedia-runtime: error=MediaFetchError: Failed to fetch media from http://127.0.0.1:<port>/bot<redacted>/file.bin: request timed out\n```\n\nWhy this covers the bug: before this PR, the Telegram media path only supplied `readIdleTimeoutMs`, which protects body stalls after a response begins. After this PR, the same `saveRemoteMedia` runtime path also receives a request deadline, so a custom Bot API/media endpoint that accepts the request but never completes headers is aborted instead of hanging inbound media processing.\n\nWhat was not tested: live Telegram Bot API traffic. This proof uses a controlled local Bot API-style stall endpoint plus the committed Telegram regression test; no bot tokens or user media are involved.\n",
        "branch": "alix/telegram-media-download-fetch-timeout",
